        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
There is a paucity of systematic studies carried out with well-defined model systems to evaluate cancer risks to astronauts from exposure to charged particles. Glioblastomas (GBM) are deadly brain tumors with very poor prognosis and are the third leading cause of cancer-related deaths among adults aged 30-50 years. Exposure to ionizing radiation is the only known risk factor for the development of these tumors. Unprecedented advances have recently been made in the genetic analysis of these tumors as part of the Human Cancer Genome Atlas Project. Five key genetic changes predominate in GBMs - loss of Ink4a, Arf, p53, and/or PTEN, and amplification of EGFR. Under the auspices of this currently-funded NASA grant, the investigator’s lab has validated sensitive mouse models with brain-targeted deletions of these genes representing progressive steps in gliomagenesis. By using genetically “pre-sensitized” models, they are capable of rapidly assessing brain tumor development after exposure to particle radiation. Their current research shows that Fe ions are 4-fold more gliomagenic than X-rays. Additionally, they have demonstrated that Fe-induced gliomagenesis is triggered by specific oncogenic events, such as MET amplification or PDGFR over-expression, that are also relevant to human GBMs. They are currently using further finessed versions of these mouse models to analyze not only the tumorigenic potential of an array of particles and doses, but also to identify the cell types in the brain that are most susceptible to transformation following charged particle exposure. Furthermore, they aimed to undertake large-scale oncogenomic studies to identify pathways that are altered in particle-induced GBMs. 
<p>
This study had the following specific aims:<ol>
<li>Evaluation of the tumorigenic potential of HZE particles using Nestin-Cre driven Ink4a/b-/-Arff/f and p53+/f Pten+/f mouse GBM models. 
<li>Identification of the “cell-of-origin” of HZE-induced gliomas using cell-type specific Cre-ERT2 mouse GBM models. 
<li>Elucidating mechanism(s) underlying HZE induced gliomagenesis using an integrated oncogenomic approach.</ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Particle beams were provided  by  the  NASA  Space Radiation Laboratory (NSRL) at Brookhaven National Laboratory. After acceleration in the AGS Booster synchrotron, particles are extracted and transported to an irradiation station on which dose monitoring and beam characterization devices and experimental samples were mounted. The dose was measured by ionization chambers—which have very low mass and affect the beam energy and composition only  slightly—and  monitored  by  a  computer-controlled  dosimetry system that automatically cuts off the beam when the specified dose is reached.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Investigators identified two cohorts of mice that do not form spontaneous gliomas but develop high grade malignancies following particle irradiation. The first cohort is deficient in the Ink4a, Ink4b, and Arf genes (Nestin-Cre; Ink4a/b-/-Arff/f) with Nestin-Cre driving brain-specific deletions of conditional alleles. The second model carries Nestin-Cre-driven heterozygous deletions of the p53 and Pten genes (Nestin-Cre; p53+/f Pten+/f). Following intracranial irradiation with 600 MeV/n Fe ions both models developed GBMs with a frequency of approximately 25%. At least four-fold higher doses of X-rays were needed to achieve similar tumor frequencies, indicating an relative biological effectiveness (RBE) of at least four for gliomagenesis induced by Fe ions. Multimodal analysis of Fe-induced mouse tumors revealed additional alterations of GBM-relevant tumor suppressor pathways, similar to that seen in humans. Amplification of the receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) Met was the most frequent oncogenic alteration in the Fe-induced tumors in the Ink4a/b-/-Arff/f model. In the p53+/-Pten+/- model, loss of heterozygosity of Pten and up-regulation of the RTK Pdgfr-a with concomitant activation of PI3K-Akt signaling was common. Both RTK up-regulation events correlated with high expression of the reprogramming transcription factor Sox2. Ex-vivo studies demonstrated the importance of Sox2 for the maintenance of a cancer stem cell phenotype in the tumor cultures. In additional studies investigators sought to determine the transforming potential of an array of charged particles with different linear energy transfer (LET)s (proton, carbon, silicon, iron) in order to improve their understanding of the risks from galactic cosmic radiation (GCR) exposure. Here, we used the Nestin-Cre; Ink4a/b-/-Arff/f mouse and demonstrated gliomagenesis following irradiation with all tested particles. Mice exposed to lighter ions (0.5 Gy of protons or carbon) formed tumors 5-10% of the time. This frequency increased up to 37.5% following exposure to silicon but then decreased with further increases in Z and LET.
<p>
No datasets exist for this study. A final report was archived.
